Figure~\ref{fig:training-isr-curves} traces training ISR across conditions.
Extended training (1000 steps) reveals that ISR $\beta{=}0.1$'s non-hacking rate at 500 steps ($17.3\%$, seed~42) is transient: by step 1000, H7 gaming escalates from $16.0\%$ to $96.7\%$ while the non-hacking rate collapses to $2.2\%$ ($n_{\text{comp}}$: $5.58{\to}2.89$).
The optimization converges to a fixed point where $\text{ISR}^{*}{=}0.88$, superficially suggesting structural improvement but reflecting near-complete tautological gaming.
This mirrors the exec-only 1000-step trajectory (\S\ref{sec:diagnostic}): both converge to ${\sim}0\%$ non-hacking proofs despite opposite ISR values ($0.88$ vs.\ $0.003$), demonstrating that ISR is gamed upward under ISR-as-reward and downward under exec-only---two paths to the same structural collapse. %% registry: plan002-b01-s42-1000, plan016-extended-1000-s42

\textbf{Micro-dose ISR ($\beta{=}0.05$): gaming at minimal signal.}
Even at $\beta{=}0.05$, the lowest dose tested (seed~42), 100\% of proved samples ($n{=}55$) are H7a tautologies with 0\% non-hacking, showing that gaming emerges at the minimum ISR signal strength. %% registry: sweep-isr-b005-s42

\textbf{Medium-dose ISR ($\beta{=}0.2$): maximum exploitation.}
At $\beta{=}0.2$, all three seeds converge to H7a tautology with $0\%$ non-hacking (pooled $n{=}290$), the maximum exploitation point in the dose-response curve.
Seeds achieve $\text{ISR}^{*}{=}0.55$--$0.96$ through single-premise-equals-conclusion constructions. %% registry: sweep-isr-b02-s42/s43/s44

\textbf{High-dose ISR ($\beta{=}0.3$): multi-modal Goodhart.}
At $\beta{=}0.3$, ISR-as-reward produces extreme cross-seed instability ($\text{PR}{=}66{\pm}42\%$, spanning 18--95\%); each seed converges to a distinct pathway (Appendix~\ref{app:detailed-results}). %% registry: plan002-additive-b03-s42/s43/s44
Seed~42: \emph{reward collapse} ($\text{PR}{=}18\%$), following the pattern described by~\citet{gao-reward-overoptimization}.
Seed~43: \emph{H1 shortcutting} ($\text{PR}{=}86\%$, $\text{H1}{=}69\%$, only $23\%$ non-hacking).
Seed~44: \emph{pure H7 gaming} ($\text{PR}{=}95\%$, $\text{H7}{=}100\%$, $\text{ISR}^{*}{=}0.742$, $0\%$ non-hacking)---simultaneously gaming \emph{both} execution and structural metrics through the same construction.
The random control at $\beta{=}0.3$ is equally unstable ($\text{PR}{=}54{\pm}35\%$, spanning 23--92\%) with near-universal H1 hacking ($91\%$, pooled) and ${\leq}4\%$ non-hacking per seed; high-dose proxy weighting destabilizes training regardless of signal source; ISR produces diverse failure modes (reward collapse, H1 shortcutting, pure H7 gaming) while random converges to H1-dominated hacking with sporadic H7 ($7\%$ pooled, $0$--$7$ H7a per seed; ISR vs.\ random H7 $\text{OR}{=}13.7$, 95\% CI $[7.0, 26.8]$) (Appendix~\ref{app:beta03}).

\subsection{Cross-Logic Diagnostic Transfer}
\label{sec:crosslogic}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\columnwidth]{figures/fig4_prolog_comparison.pdf}
\caption{Cross-logic results. \textbf{Left}: FOL vs.\ Prolog exec-only $\text{ISR}^*$ across 3 seeds (s44 Prolog $0.387$ is a small-sample artifact, $n{=}21$). \textbf{Right}: Prolog ISR-reward gaming-mode composition---all 3 seeds reach 0\% non-hacking through distinct mechanisms.}
\label{fig:prolog-comparison}
\end{figure}

We replicate the FOL pipeline with Prolog/SWI-Prolog: (i) does ISR retain diagnostic value on a structurally different logic; (ii) does ISR-as-reward reproduce the Goodhart effects observed in FOL?
Detailed per-seed numbers are in Appendix Table~\ref{tab:prolog-detailed}.

\textbf{Prolog exec-only: ISR catches redundancy across logics.}
Two of three seeds (s42, s43) show $\text{ISR}^{*}{\approx}0$ despite moderate-to-high PR (0.83, 0.56). %% registry: plan006-prolog-exec-s42, plan006-prolog-exec-s43-r3
Inspection reveals \emph{redundant coding}: ground facts repeated multiple times (e.g., \texttt{penguin(fido).} appearing three times), so per-clause LOO never breaks the proof.
This is a different failure semantics from FOL exec-only (conclusion embedding), but $\text{ISR}^{*}$ flags both.
We are careful to distinguish: exec-only \emph{low} ISR signals \emph{redundancy}; ISR-rewarded \emph{high} ISR signals \emph{metric gaming} (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy-method})---the same number does not license the same reasoning-quality conclusion.

\textbf{Prolog ISR-as-reward: consistent gaming across all seeds.}
The Prolog result is qualitatively consistent with FOL: 3/3 seeds reach $0\%$ non-hacking via three distinct mechanisms (Figure~\ref{fig:prolog-comparison}).
Seed~42: clean H7 tautologies ($\text{ISR}^{*}{=}1.0$, all $50/100$ proved). %% registry: plan006-prolog-sta-s42
Seed~43: \emph{degenerate uppercase pseudo-Prolog} (e.g., \texttt{MARY\_IS\_AN\_AVA\-DEOTE.}); strict text-match returns $\text{H7}{=}0$ but functional analysis shows all 38 proved samples have $n_{\text{necessary}}{=}1$ (functionally H7). %% registry: plan006-prolog-sta-s43
Seed~44: \emph{query-as-fact gaming}---the model transforms the NL question into a snake-case atom asserted as both fact and query (e.g., \texttt{is\_the\_dog\_furry.}), with $95/97$ proved samples at $n_{\text{necessary}}{=}1$. %% registry: plan006-prolog-sta-s44
Seed~44 evaded our initial strict classifier (reported $100\%$ non-hacking); functional cross-validation exposed it---a methodological lesson that text-match classifiers have blind spots and STA's necessity profile is the cross-check.
We attribute Prolog's complete collapse to its simpler operational semantics: a broader gaming basin makes the non-hacking residual disappear.
This is a qualitative observation from a single configuration (Prolog + ISR $\beta{=}0.1$, 3 seeds); generalization to other logic types requires further experimentation.

\subsection{Hacking Taxonomy}
\label{sec:taxonomy}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.92\columnwidth]{figures/fig3_hacking_composition.pdf}
\caption{Hacking composition across reward conditions (proved samples; exec-only: seed~42; all other bars: pooled across 3 seeds, per-seed breakdown in Appendix Table~\ref{tab:fol-detailed}). ISR converts H1 into more extreme H7 without reducing total hacking; SFT shows near-zero hacking.}
\label{fig:hacking-composition}
\end{figure}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Mode} & \textbf{Exec} & \textbf{ISR$_{.1}$} & \textbf{Rnd$_{.1}$} & \textbf{SFT} & \textbf{Prolog$_{\text{ISR}}$} \\
\midrule
H1 embed       & 87 & 18 & 71 & 7 & 0$^\sharp$ \\
H7 duplicate   & 0  & 71 & 14 & 0 & 100$^\sharp$ \\
H8 prem-subset & 6  & 5  & 8   & 0 & --- \\
Tautological   & --- & --- & --- & --- & s42 \\
Repetition     & s43 & --- & --- & --- & --- \\
Contradiction  & 1k & --- & --- & --- & --- \\
Cmplx.\ vacuous& 0  & 0  & 4  & 0 & --- \\
Query-as-fact  & --- & --- & --- & --- & s44 \\
\midrule
\textbf{Non-hacking} & 8  & 6  & 10 & \textbf{92} & \textbf{0} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Eight hacking modes across conditions (\% of proved samples; exec-only/SFT: seed~42; ISR/Random: pooled across 3 seeds). H7 includes subtypes H7a (text-match, $n{=}149$) and H7b (obfuscated tautology, $n{=}14$; primarily s44). Cells with seed identifiers indicate the mode dominates that seed. $^\sharp$Prolog row pools strict+functional (\S\ref{sec:crosslogic}).}
\label{tab:hacking-taxonomy}
\end{table}

The eight modes (Table~\ref{tab:hacking-taxonomy}; definitions in Appendix~\ref{app:hacking-modes}) span 2 logic types and 6 reward conditions.
The three dominant modes---\textbf{H1} (conclusion embedding, GRPO-universal at $68$--$100\%$), \textbf{H7} (conclusion duplication, ISR-amplified ${\sim}5\times$; Fisher $p{=}1.3{\times}10^{-39}$), and \textbf{H8} (premise-subset gaming; Appendix~\ref{app:h8})---account for $>90\%$ of hacking.
H7 includes an obfuscated subtype \textbf{H7b} ($14/231$) that evades text-match classifiers via vacuous quantifier wrapping, simultaneously gaming execution reward, ISR, and the hacking detector (\S\ref{sec:discussion}).
The cross-condition pattern: \emph{reward formulation determines hacking composition but not prevalence}---all GRPO conditions show $\geq 80\%$ pooled hacking; only SFT achieves $92.3\%$ non-hacking.
No GRPO condition produces a high-PR, majority non-hacking run; the taxonomy is descriptive, not exhaustive (query-as-fact gaming was discovered only via functional cross-validation).

